From: Rasheed <99349713+gitRasheed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:42:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/61] NNS.ARMA.optim: evaluate nonlin and both when
 linear.approximation = FALSE

Without the linear approximation the nonlin and both stages computed no
objective at all: nns.estimates.indiv stayed numeric(), min() of it is
Inf, and those methods could never win, so linear.approximation = FALSE
silently returned the lin-only answer (in 0.02 s). The docs describe
FALSE as 'a more exhaustive search over the objective space'.

Score every candidate seasonal set with NNS.ARMA(method = j) in those
stages, using the same greedy path search as the lin stage.

Before -> after (AirPassengers[1:132], h = 12,
seasonal.factor = c(6,12,18,24), linear.approximation = FALSE):
  method lin,  periods 12,     obj.fn 0.07894 (= lin.only = TRUE)
  method both, periods 12 24,  obj.fn 0.04621 (= Python nns_arma_optim)
linear.approximation = TRUE (the default) is unchanged.
---
 R/ARMA_optim.R | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/R/ARMA_optim.R b/R/ARMA_optim.R
index a086f27..eda47b0 100644
--- a/R/ARMA_optim.R
+++ b/R/ARMA_optim.R
@@ -235,6 +235,19 @@ NNS.ARMA.optim <- function(variable,
         nns.estimates.indiv <- unlist(nns.estimates.indiv)
       }
       
+      if(j != "lin" && !linear.approximation){
+        # Exhaustive search: score every candidate seasonal set with the
+        # method itself (previously nothing was evaluated here, so nonlin and
+        # both could never be selected).
+        nns.estimates.indiv <- unlist(lapply(1:ncol(seasonal.combs[[i]]), function(k) {
+          actual <- tail(variable, h_eval)
+          predicted <- NNS.ARMA(variable, training.set = training.set, h = h_eval,
+                                seasonal.factor = seasonal.combs[[i]][, k], method = j,
+                                plot = FALSE, negative.values = negative.values)
+          eval(obj.fn)
+        }))
+      }
+      
       if(j=="nonlin" && linear.approximation){
         # Find the min (obj.fn) for a given seasonals sequence
         actual <- tail(variable, h_eval)
